  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Composite functions"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What is a composite function?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- A **composite function **is where one function is applied after another function"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/06/2.8.1-Language-of-Functions-Notes-Diagram-8.png",
   "file": "assets/3dd120f9b69f-2-8-1-language-of-functions-notes-diagram-8.png",
   "alt": "Language of Functions Notes Diagram 8, A Level & AS Level Pure Maths Revision Notes",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The ‘**output’** of one function will be the ‘**input’** of the next one\n- Sometimes called **function-of-a-function**\n- A composite function can be denoted\n\n  - $fg(x)$\n  - $f(g(x))$\n  - `f open square brackets g left parenthesis x right parenthesis close square brackets`\n  - $(f\\circg)(x)$\n  - All of these mean “$f$ of $g(x)$”"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/06/2.8.2-Composite-Functions-Notes-Diagram-1.png",
   "file": "assets/4da8ed2c98f3-2-8-2-composite-functions-notes-diagram-1.png",
   "alt": "Composite Functions Notes Diagram 1, A Level & AS Level Pure Maths Revision Notes",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I use composite functions?"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/06/2.8.2-Composite-Functions-Notes-Diagram-2.png",
   "file": "assets/6871fdd6407a-2-8-2-composite-functions-notes-diagram-2.png",
   "alt": "Composite Functions Notes Diagram 2, A Level & AS Level Pure Maths Revision Notes",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- **Recognise** the notation The order matters\n\n  - **fg(x)** means **“f **of** g **of** x”**\n\n  - First apply *g *to *x *to get $g(x)$\n  - Then apply *f* to the previous output to get $f(g(x))$\n  - Always start with the function **closest to the variable**\n  - $fg(x)$ is not usually equal to $gf(x)$"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What are special cases of composite functions?"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/06/2.8.2-Composite-Functions-Notes-Diagram-3.png",
   "file": "assets/4ee894a88c77-2-8-2-composite-functions-notes-diagram-3.png",
   "alt": "Composite Functions Notes Diagram 3, A Level & AS Level Pure Maths Revision Notes",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- **fg(x)** and **gf(x)** are generally different but can sometimes be the same\n- **ff(x)** is written as **f**<sup>**2**</sup>**(x)**\n- Inverse functions **ff**<sup>**-1**</sup>**(x) = f**<sup>**-1**</sup>**f(x) = x**"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "- **Domain** and **range** are important.In **fg(x)**, the ‘output’ (range) of **g** **must** be in the domain of **f(x), s**o **fg(x)** could exist, but **gf(x)** may not (or not for some values of x)."
  },
  {
